Thank you for adding the known-edge simulations and computational-cost comparisons. These results strengthen the experimental evaluation, but I do not believe the original concerns regarding validity and scalability have been fully resolved.

1. Limited scale of the synthetic networks

The new simulations contain only 3–5 ROIs, with at most 20 candidate directed edges, and are restricted to relatively simple common-driver, chain, and diamond-DAG structures. These experiments are useful as preliminary validation, but they remain far from realistic brain networks containing tens or hundreds of ROIs.

Several key metrics also show substantial variability across the 50 realizations, particularly in the common-driver settings. Moreover, larger networks introduce multiple confounders, indirect paths, feedback loops, modular structure, hidden nodes, and heterogeneous hemodynamic effects. Since the proposed method models each directed ROI pair independently, these factors may become increasingly problematic at larger scales.
